package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	names := []string{"Ava", "Eve", "Leo", "Max", "Tom"}
	more := []string{"Zed", "Kim"}
	fmt.Println("first:", names[:3])
	fmt.Println("last:", names[len(names)-2:])
	fmt.Println("middle:", names[1:3])

	names = names[:len(names)-1]
	names = append(names, more...)
	fmt.Println("joined:", names)
}

steps.body:
The `sort` package sorts slices from smallest to largest. Add it to your imports:

```go harness
package main

{{code}}

func main() {
	s := []int{70, 95, 82, 64, 88}
	sort.Ints(s)
	fmt.Println(s)
}
```

```go
import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)
```

Use the function that matches the element type: `sort.Ints` for `[]int`, `sort.Float64s` for `[]float64`, and `sort.Strings` for `[]string`. They sort **in place** and return nothing, so call them on their own line. `s = sort.Ints(s)` does not compile. Because slices share their elements, any other name for the same slice sees the new order too.

After sorting, `s[0]` is the smallest value and `s[len(s)-1]` the largest.

Gotcha: strings sort by byte order, not alphabetically. Every uppercase letter comes before every lowercase one, so `"Zoo"` sorts before `"apple"`.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func main() {
	scores := []int{70, 95, 82, 64, 88}
	prices := []float64{4.5, 1.25, 3.75}
	words := []string{"pear", "Apple", "fig"}

	sort.Ints(scores)
	sort.Float64s(prices)
	sort.Strings(words)

	fmt.Println(scores)
	fmt.Println(prices)
	fmt.Println(words)
	fmt.Println("lowest:", scores[0])
	fmt.Println("highest:", scores[len(scores)-1])
}

